292
00:16:38,360 --> 00:16:41,380
Cadillac built roughly 2,000 of these limousines a year.

293
00:16:42,320 --> 00:16:44,000
And although these numbers were inadequate

294
00:16:44,001 --> 00:16:45,320
for financial success,

295
00:16:45,680 --> 00:16:47,740
the car was a marketing tool for Cadillac

296
00:16:47,741 --> 00:16:49,860
and for Cary International.

297
00:16:50,840 --> 00:16:53,260
Cary's rapidly expanding limousine empire

298
00:16:53,261 --> 00:16:55,200
was on its way to becoming the largest

299
00:16:55,201 --> 00:16:58,040
and only publicly traded limo company in the world.

300
00:16:59,320 --> 00:17:01,160
As Cary's business expanded,

301
00:17:01,680 --> 00:17:05,520
He turned to the Series 75 to standardize his fleet,

302
00:17:05,521 --> 00:17:09,180
and Cadillac turned to him to test their product.

303
00:17:09,181 --> 00:17:12,180
The 1956 had a dog of a transmission.

304
00:17:12,181 --> 00:17:14,520
They would send a whole bunch of transmissions to carry.

305
00:17:14,521 --> 00:17:15,680
They would just replace them.

306
00:17:15,681 --> 00:17:18,020
They had one that lasted 80,000 miles.

307
00:17:18,021 --> 00:17:19,340
And so Cadillac said, send it to us.

308
00:17:19,341 --> 00:17:21,180
Take it out, and it's oil and everything.

309
00:17:21,181 --> 00:17:23,340
We want to see what went right.

310
00:17:23,341 --> 00:17:27,220
And so they helped the company develop a better transmission.

311
00:17:27,221 --> 00:17:29,180
With the gas crisis of the 70s

312
00:17:29,181 --> 00:17:33,020
in increasing consciousness of the bottom line, Cadillac

313
00:17:33,021 --> 00:17:38,140
discontinued the Series 75 in 1976 and replaced it with a

314
00:17:38,141 --> 00:17:41,980
downsized limousine the following year.

315
00:17:41,981 --> 00:17:44,940
Ironically, this proved to be an opportunity for even bigger

316
00:17:44,941 --> 00:17:48,380
limousines to dominate American roads.

317
00:17:48,381 --> 00:17:51,420
The demise of the factory limousine created a market for

318
00:17:51,421 --> 00:17:55,980
custom stretch limousines. But with the economic boom of the

319
00:17:55,981 --> 00:17:59,980
the chauffeured transportation industry continued to grow.

320
00:18:00,080 --> 00:18:02,680
When Solombrino joined Davell in the early 80s,

321
00:18:02,780 --> 00:18:05,220
there were 1,000 companies in the country.

322
00:18:05,320 --> 00:18:10,420
By 1990, that number had grown to 12,000.

323
00:18:10,520 --> 00:18:12,460
In the corporate world, the limousine had become

324
00:18:12,560 --> 00:18:16,340
both a status symbol and a safety tool.

325
00:18:16,440 --> 00:18:18,500
If you're a big corporation and you're doing a party

326
00:18:18,600 --> 00:18:21,940
for 300 people, you're going to make sure that those 300 people

327
00:18:22,040 --> 00:18:24,980
have a ride home because you don't want your corporation

328
00:18:24,981 --> 00:18:27,680
corporation to have the liability of having an accident

329
00:18:27,780 --> 00:18:30,820
of someone who might have been drinking at your corporate event.

330
00:18:30,920 --> 00:18:35,620
Next, since Cadillac no longer makes a factory limousine,

331
00:18:35,720 --> 00:18:37,720
how does this become this?

332
00:18:41,800 --> 00:18:46,140
In the late 1940s and 1950s, the Cadillac Series 75

333
00:18:46,240 --> 00:18:48,440
accounted for 85% of the limousines

334
00:18:48,540 --> 00:18:51,240
built in the United States.

335
00:18:51,340 --> 00:18:54,740
Limousines will return on Modern Marvels.

336
00:18:54,980 --> 00:19:04,580
Stretch limousines.

337
00:19:05,200 --> 00:19:07,040
They're the biggest cars on the road.

